WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.933
This is a reproducibility study of Graph Edit Networks,

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:11.407
a graph neural network output layer that predicts
how a graph changes over time as a sequence of interpretable edits.

00:00:11.487 --> 00:00:17.220
The reproduction re-implements the model and
re-tests the paper's four experimental claims.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.655
Graph time-series prediction asks a model to
forecast the next graph in a sequence,

00:00:21.735 --> 00:00:25.790
not just a label.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.925
Standard graph networks emit node or edge probabilities,

00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:34.060
which can't naturally express the structural
operations that transform one graph into the next.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.430
Graph Edit Networks close this gap by predicting an
explicit,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.232
human-readable edit script.

00:00:40.312 --> 00:00:45.836
The original work claims it beats every baseline,
reaches perfect accuracy on trees,

00:00:45.916 --> 00:00:51.308
and scales to large graphs, but those claims rest
on briefly-described benchmarks,

00:00:51.388 --> 00:00:54.110
where independent reproduction adds value.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:01:00.339
The reproduction contributes four things: it
re-runs the model and baseline to check each claim;

00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:04.107
it documents the synthetic data generators the
paper omitted;

00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:07.813
it adds a cleaner setup separating training and
test series;

00:01:07.893 --> 00:01:12.630
and it shows some benchmarks let the model win by
memorising seen transitions.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:19.455
A graph edit network attaches to a standard GNN
backbone and predicts a script of edits: insert,

00:01:19.535 --> 00:01:23.126
delete or replace a node, or insert or delete an edge.

00:01:23.206 --> 00:01:27.272
Applying them in sequence maps the current graph to
the next.

00:01:27.352 --> 00:01:32.166
Training uses reference mappings from
graph-edit-distance approximators,

00:01:32.246 --> 00:01:35.429
with two loss variants, hinge and cross-entropy,

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:38.080
and edge-filtering for citation graphs.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.160
Benchmarks span three families: dynamical graph
systems like Edit Cycles,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.125
Degree Rules and Game of Life;

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:50.691
tree systems for Boolean-formula simplification and
Peano addition; and, for scaling,

00:01:50.771 --> 00:01:56.453
an arXiv citation network of about twenty-seven
thousand papers yielding fifteen hundred

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.400
sub-graphs up to nearly three thousand nodes.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:07.324
Three of the four original claims hold: the model
beats the variational-autoencoder baseline on every dynamical task,

00:02:07.404 --> 00:02:12.862
reaches near-perfect accuracy on trees, and its
forward pass grows sub-quadratically.

00:02:12.942 --> 00:02:17.098
The fourth fails: backward passes were claimed to
scale linearly,

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:20.030
but the fitted exponent is clearly above one.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:26.717
Two robustness checks: swapping the ad-hoc
initialisation for Erdős–Rényi and configuration-model

00:02:26.797 --> 00:02:29.153
generators barely changes any metric,

00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:33.170
and adding a proper held-out test set lowers scores
slightly.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.833
But a deeper diagnostic finds the tree generators
mostly produce unsimplifiable trees,

00:02:38.913 --> 00:02:44.364
only thirteen percent of Boolean and twenty-six
percent of Peano samples are usable,

00:02:44.444 --> 00:02:46.010
undercutting those tasks.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.466
The verdict is nuanced: Graph Edit Networks are
reproducible,

00:02:50.546 --> 00:02:53.736
elegant and interpretable, and most claims hold.

00:02:53.816 --> 00:02:58.164
But one scaling claim is wrong, backward passes are
super-linear,

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:04.500
and several benchmarks reward memorising seen
transitions rather than genuine generalisation.
